## Proof

**Answer leak check:** The problem statement contains only the problem; no solution, sketch, or answer is leaked.

---

**Claim.** The entire functions $f$ satisfying $|f(z)|\le|g(z)|$ for all $z\in\mathbb C$ are exactly
$$f(z)=c\,g(z),\qquad c\in\mathbb C,\ |c|\le 1.$$

---

### Case 1: $g\equiv 0$.

Then $|f(z)|\le 0$ forces $f\equiv 0=c\cdot g$ for any $c$ with $|c|\le 1$. ✓

### Case 2: $g\not\equiv 0$.

**Step 1 — Zeros of $g$ are zeros of $f$ of at least the same order.**

Let $z_0$ be a zero of $g$ of order $m\ge 1$. From $|f(z_0)|\le|g(z_0)|=0$ we get $f(z_0)=0$. Write
$$g(z)=(z-z_0)^m\,g_1(z),\quad g_1(z_0)\ne 0,\qquad f(z)=(z-z_0)^n\,f_1(z),\quad f_1(z_0)\ne 0$$
(with $n=\infty$ if $f\equiv 0$). For $z\ne z_0$ near $z_0$,
$$\frac{|f(z)|}{|g(z)|}=|z-z_0|^{n-m}\,\frac{|f_1(z)|}{|g_1(z)|}\le 1.$$
If $n<m$, the factor $|z-z_0|^{n-m}\to\infty$ while $|f_1(z_0)|/|g_1(z_0)|>0$, contradicting the bound. Hence $n\ge m$.

**Step 2 — $h:=f/g$ extends to an entire function.**

The only possible singularities of the meromorphic function $f/g$ are at the zeros of $g$. By Step 1, every zero of $g$ of order $m$ is a zero of $f$ of order $n\ge m$, so the singularity is removable. Thus $h$ is entire.

**Step 3 — $h$ is bounded, hence constant.**

Wherever $g(z)\ne 0$, we have $|h(z)|=|f(z)|/|g(z)|\le 1$. The zero set of $g$ is discrete, so by continuity of the extended $h$,
$$|h(z_0)|=\lim_{z\to z_0}|h(z)|\le 1$$
at every zero $z_0$ of $g$ as well. Therefore $|h(z)|\le 1$ for all $z\in\mathbb C$. By Liouville's theorem, $h\equiv c$ for some constant $c$ with $|c|\le 1$.

**Step 4 — Conclusion.**

Since $h=f/g\equiv c$, we have $f=c\,g$ with $|c|\le 1$. Conversely, any such $f$ clearly satisfies $|f(z)|=|c|\,|g(z)|\le|g(z)|$.

---

Both cases are unified by the single statement: $f=c\,g$ for a constant $c$ with $|c|\le 1$.

### PROOF COMPLETE
